  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 1222 AM CDT Sun Sep 13 2026\n\nHeat remains the main concern for the next few days. Heat indices up \nto 114 are forecast across many locations this afternoon. Thus, a \nHeat Advisory has been issued from Noon to 7 pm for most of S TX. \nMajor to extreme risk of heat related impacts will continue through \nthe middle of next week and Heat Advisories may be needed at times. \nBy the latter half of the week, models show slightly cooler max \ntemps, but still running above normal.\n\nRain chances increase slightly today in response to a ridge being \nnudged northward as an inverted trough shifts northwest across S TX. \nThis will allow for a slightly more active sea breeze with a 15-30% \nof convection. A few may even reach the Brush Country. The trough \naxis will be over the TX Coastal Waters leading to higher PoPs (25-\n50%) over the gulf waters. Similar conditions expected Monday with \nthe mid level trough slowly lifting northwestward around the \nsouthwest periphery of the mid level ridge of high pressure. Models \nindicate the ridge shifts back across S TX Tuesday, decreasing the \nrain chances. \n\nBy mid week, we could see some relief from the heat and lack of \nrain. Models have been indicating an increase in rain chances \nbeginning mid week in response to a surge of deeper moisture from \nthe gulf waters and a short wave tracking around the southern \nperiphery of the mid level high pressure centered over the eastern \nCONUS. Highest chances (30-65% across the eastern half of S TX) are \nexpected Thursday when the deepest moisture with 2.1-2.4 inch PWATS \nadvects across S TX. Models begin to diverge by the end of the work \nweek with the GFS indicating much drier air moving into S TX as the \ncenter of the high moves southward over S TX, while the Canadian and \nECMWF show the center of the high shifting over Mexico allowing for \ndeep moisture to remain over S TX. For now, will continue with a low \nto medium chance Friday through next weekend.\n\nOne other concern to be monitored next week will be higher than \nnormal tides and/or an increased rip current risk. Models show a \nlong easterly fetch developing across the northern gulf waters by \nmid week which may help push water farther up the beaches. At this \ntime, minor coastal flooding is not anticipated but will continue to \nbe monitored.",
